Here is a simple MATLAB program to convert an ultrasound time-domain signal  
**Voltage vs Time** into **Magnitude (dB) vs Frequency (MHz)** using the FFT.

## MATLAB code

```matlab
% ---------------------------------------------------------
% Convert ultrasound signal: Voltage vs Time
% to frequency spectrum: Magnitude (dB) vs Frequency (MHz)
% ---------------------------------------------------------

clear; clc; close all;

%% Load or define your data
% Example: replace these with your actual time and voltage vectors
% time should be in seconds
% voltage should be in volts

% Example synthetic data:
Fs = 100e6;                    % Sampling frequency = 100 MHz
dt = 1/Fs;
time = 0:dt:10e-6-dt;          % 10 microseconds
voltage = sin(2*pi*5e6*time) + 0.5*sin(2*pi*12e6*time);

% If you have data in a file, use something like:
% data = readmatrix('ultrasound_data.csv');
% time = data(:,1);      % seconds
% voltage = data(:,2);   % volts

%% Make sure vectors are columns
time = time(:);
voltage = voltage(:);

%% Compute sampling frequency from time vector
dt = mean(diff(time));         % Time step
Fs = 1/dt;                     % Sampling frequency
N = length(voltage);           % Number of samples

%% Optional: remove DC offset
voltage = voltage - mean(voltage);

%% Optional: apply a window to reduce spectral leakage
window = hann(N);
voltage_win = voltage .* window;

%% FFT
Y = fft(voltage_win);

%% Take only the positive frequency half
N_half = floor(N/2) + 1;
Y_half = Y(1:N_half);

%% Frequency axis in Hz, then convert to MHz
f = (0:N_half-1)*(Fs/N);
f_MHz = f / 1e6;

%% Magnitude spectrum
mag = abs(Y_half);

% Correct amplitude roughly for window loss
mag = mag / sum(window);

% Convert to dB
mag_dB = 20*log10(mag + eps);   % eps avoids log(0)

%% Plot time-domain signal
figure;
plot(time*1e6, voltage, 'b');
xlabel('Time (\mus)');
ylabel('Voltage (V)');
title('Ultrasound Signal: Voltage vs Time');
grid on;

%% Plot frequency-domain signal
figure;
plot(f_MHz, mag_dB, 'r', 'LineWidth', 1.5);
xlabel('Frequency (MHz)');
ylabel('Magnitude (dB)');
title('Ultrasound Spectrum: Magnitude (dB) vs Frequency (MHz)');
grid on;
xlim([0 max(f_MHz)]);
```

---

## If your data is already in a CSV file

Suppose your file has:

- column 1 = time in seconds
- column 2 = voltage in volts

Use this version:

```matlab
clear; clc; close all;

%% Read data
data = readmatrix('ultrasound_data.csv');
time = data(:,1);
voltage = data(:,2);

%% Ensure column vectors
time = time(:);
voltage = voltage(:);

%% Sampling info
dt = mean(diff(time));
Fs = 1/dt;
N = length(voltage);

%% Remove DC
voltage = voltage - mean(voltage);

%% Windowing
window = hann(N);
voltage_win = voltage .* window;

%% FFT
Y = fft(voltage_win);

%% Positive frequencies only
N_half = floor(N/2) + 1;
Y_half = Y(1:N_half);

%% Frequency axis
f = (0:N_half-1)*(Fs/N);
f_MHz = f / 1e6;

%% Magnitude in dB
mag = abs(Y_half) / sum(window);
mag_dB = 20*log10(mag + eps);

%% Plot
figure;
subplot(2,1,1);
plot(time*1e6, voltage, 'b');
xlabel('Time (\mus)');
ylabel('Voltage (V)');
title('Voltage vs Time');
grid on;

subplot(2,1,2);
plot(f_MHz, mag_dB, 'r', 'LineWidth', 1.5);
xlabel('Frequency (MHz)');
ylabel('Magnitude (dB)');
title('Magnitude (dB) vs Frequency (MHz)');
grid on;
xlim([0 max(f_MHz)]);
```

---

## Notes

### 1. dB reference
The line

```matlab
mag_dB = 20*log10(mag + eps);
```

gives dB relative to **1 volt** amplitude approximately.

If you want the spectrum normalized to the maximum value (so the peak is 0 dB), use:

```matlab
mag_dB = 20*log10(mag / max(mag) + eps);
```

Then the plot will show **relative magnitude in dB**.

---

### 2. Why use a window?
A Hann window reduces spectral leakage, which is useful for ultrasound signals that are often pulsed.

---

### 3. Frequency units
The FFT gives frequency in Hz. This line converts it to MHz:

```matlab
f_MHz = f / 1e6;
```

---

If you want, I can also give you:

1. a version for **A-scan ultrasound data**,  
2. a version that reads **Excel files**, or  
3. a version that computes **power spectrum in dB** instead of amplitude spectrum.

